Subject: [PATCH 097/119] fix: ambient light entities were pre-exposed, and
 r_HDRDebug 1 now says which contributors the exposure reaches
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Follow-up report from the first runtime test: with the switch on, the aperture
moves sunlit surfaces and barely moves shadowed ones. Audited every contributor
against the three delivery routes the pre-exposure has - the
m_fAdaptedSceneScaleLBuffer sites, CSkyStage's own multiplies, and the per-view
CV_SceneExposure the forward/emissive writers read. Full table in
scene-notes/research/s8-exposure-coverage.md.

One real bug, fixed here. TiledLightVolumes.cpp applied the latched scale to
EVERY light list, ambient lights (list 1) included. In TiledShading.cfi an
ambient light is not an emitter:

    dirFactor = lightColor > 1 ? NdotL * lightOccl : saturate(PdotL);
    lightTerm = lerp(1, lightColor, attenuation * filter * dirFactor);
    diffuseAcc *= lightTerm;  specularAcc *= lightTerm;

a MODULATOR over an accumulator that already carries the pre-exposure. Exposing
its colour does two wrong things at once: it applies the exposure a second time
inside the lerp, and it moves the `lightColor > 1` decision boundary, so at a
daylight pre-exposure of 0.29 a brightening ambient of 1.5 arrives as 0.44 and
silently flips to the darkening branch. FXConstantDefs.cfi's SCENE_BRDF_NORM_FACTOR
comment had already reasoned this out and claimed ambient lights were guarded in
TiledLightVolumes.cpp; they were not. List 1 now takes an intensity scale of
exactly 1.0. Probes keep theirs - their cube is baked at unity, so the tint
multiply exposes it exactly once. Off the switch the scale is 1.0 in both
branches, so the stock picture is bit-identical.

The likely shadow filler is NOT that, and is not fixed here because it is S8's:
SVOGI. ApplyGI() in integration modes 1 and 2 REPLACES the pre-exposed probe
diffuse (and in mode 2 the specular) with the GI buffers, and D3D_SVO.cpp carries
no scene-referred code at all - so with GI on, every bit of indirect diffuse is
exposure-invariant while the sun tracks the aperture, which is the symptom
exactly. Global fog's inscatter is the second candidate, on the same footing.
Both need a units decision (the voxel pool's RGBM 4.0 cap; where in the fog chain
the multiply belongs), not a one-liner, and D13 says let them break visibly.

So the frame says which is which. r_HDRDebug 1 gains two coverage lines computed
from live switch and cvar state - what the pre-exposure reaches, what it does not
and is currently ON, with the cvar to switch each off named inline. A cross means
"still on the S8 list", not "broken". This exists because an exposure-invariant
shadow filler looks like nothing at all in the picture: the aperture simply stops
working in the dark half of the frame, and there is no other clue.

Also corrected, no code: the checklist's C2 expectation. Lowering the camera's WB
Kelvin under daylight correctly makes the picture COOLER - the number says what
the camera is told the illuminant IS, so telling a daylight scene "the light is
tungsten" makes it correct the other way. The acceptance is that clipped
highlights stay neutral, not that the picture warms. SceneReferredSpec.md section
8 item 3 and the §7 acceptance line, and docs/SceneReferredLook.md's symptom
table, now say so; README already had it right.

